2026-08-27T20:10Z claude note X1 TIMING HALF MEASURED (bench/slab_scan.mjs -> bench/results/0025-x1-timing/slab-vs-rows.json, synthetic unit vectors dim 384, stored-norm dot, container CPU — the weaker substrate, so a pass is conservative). Full-scan top-30 at 650 000 vectors: per-row BLOB rows cost 3622,6 ms median — 9x past the 400 ms clause — while the float32 slab costs 351,1 ms and the int8 slab 310,4 ms, both under it. At 100 000: rows 466,9 ms, slab 43,2 ms, int8 scans in 41,1 ms. DECISION (timing clause only): the derived-sidecar slab is confirmed as what meets the budget — it is the difference between passing and missing by 9x, not an optimization; int8 passes timing but its ship decision still waits on the recall half (real vectors, workstation). Slab and rows returned identical top-30s, the harness sanity check
2026-08-27T20:45Z claude note X4 MEASURED on the honestly-rescoped synthetic corpus (bench/constrained_match.mjs -> bench/results/0025-x4-constrained-match/synthetic-477k.json; 477 512 passages, Zipf vocabulary, no FTS optimize since upstream never issues one; container CPU). Unconstrained bm25 over the whole corpus costs 138,4 ms median; at a 1000-rowid scope the constrained query costs 3863 ms median — 26x past the 150 ms rule — and the curve is superlinear, reaching 731584 ms median at 100000 rowids. DECISION per the rule: even 1k exceeds the allowance, so NO constrained step ships and the ladder ends at the honest R18 give-up. The finding is structural: FTS5's plan for rowid IN (SELECT value FROM json_each(?)) under MATCH is worse than ranking everything, superlinearly in scope size — if scoped issue A ever wants scoping it needs a different mechanism (post-filter of ranked results, or an indexed temp table), which is a NEW experiment, not a retry. Real-corpus confirmation on the workstation remains the formality before any of this is said upstream
2026-08-29T07:58Z X2 MEASURED and it FAILS (bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/x2-verdict.json + both arms' driver output; REAL substrate, host doudou, 477 512 passages of which 465 110 full text). Warm p95 stopword-less = 1 773,0 ms against the ~500 ms rule — 3,5x over. A stock-upstream control arm on the SAME index and the SAME twenty queries settles the attribution: v1.9.0 with its stoplist intact answers them at a warm p95 of 392,3 ms, inside the allowance, so the deletion multiplies p95 by 4,5 and the median by 5,7 (1 233,2 vs 215,7 ms). The cheapest of the twenty still costs 885,3 ms, so this is not a tail artifact. DECISION per DESIGN §3: the deletion does NOT ship alone — corpus-driven pruning of >50%-df query terms is a precondition of the PR, not its follow-up. Ticket 0014's scope grows accordingly
2026-08-29T07:59Z SUBSTRATE MAP CORRECTION: the map said the 477k index 'lives on the author's machine', which is true and no longer sufficient. Every index under /home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/ (sqlite-uncapped 477 512, migrate/cmp 360 811, vectest) is pre-rename schema — 'passages' is itself the FTS5 virtual table there, while v1.9.0 expects a plain 'passages' table with 'passages_fts' beside it. Current code dies on open with 'virtual tables may not be indexed'. So X2, X3a and X4's real-corpus confirmation each need a REBUILD first, not just the author's machine: 263,7 s and 1 755,6 MiB on doudou, reproducing the old geometry exactly (477 512 / 465 110). Budget it into every remaining workstation experiment
2026-08-29T08:00Z Precision on the previous entry: X3a does not need a rebuild of an existing index — it builds its own (run_build on the monster document), so the schema break only costs it the option of reusing an on-disk dir. X4's real-corpus arm genuinely does need one: bench/constrained_match.mjs queries 'passages_fts' by name, which exists only in the new schema. Verified by reading the driver rather than assumed. Convenient consequence: the new-schema 477 512-passage index built for X2 now sits at /home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/x2-rebuild, so X4-real can run without paying the rebuild again
2026-08-29T08:34Z X2 annex, two costs the PR does not mention and the lexical/semantic reading. (a) bm25.ts addDoc() runs tokenize() over the DOCUMENT too, so on the memory backend the deletion is symmetric and in-memory postings grow by 21,8% in tokens, tf and df entries — unmeasured (X2 ran on sqlite) and material at a build already peaking near 1,8 GiB. (b) makeSnippet() centres the excerpt on the EARLIEST matching query token, and 'the' occurs near the start of nearly every passage, so excerpts collapse onto passage openings — demonstrated: stock returns the sentence carrying 'elasticity of substitution', the deleted arm returns the paper's boilerplate first line. Fusion is rrf(k=60) over a 3x-limit pool (index-manager.ts:1131), rank-based, so bm25 scores never reach it and an 88%-preserved order moves the fused result even less. tokenize() never touches the semantic path (it embeds the raw string), and the two paths run in SEQUENCE, so 1 773,0 ms is a floor for default 'auto' mode, not a ceiling
2026-08-29T08:34Z X2 MECHANISM ANNEX (bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/x2-mechanism.json). Where the cost comes from: the 29 stoplist words are 29 of 639 888 distinct terms — 0,005% of the vocabulary — and 21,8% of all token occurrences (20 153 505 of 92 623 305). 'the' sits in 84,4% of passages. Over the twenty queries, postings walked rise from 4 207 588 to 155 751 186, a factor of 37,0, while time rises only 5,6x: FTS5 walks a long list far more cheaply per entry (1 063,8 ns -> 159,6 ns), so this is a design cost, not an engine defect to optimise away. What the cost BUYS: mean top-20 overlap between the arms is 88,1%, 19 of 20 queries keep the same first hit, 11 of 20 return an identical list. The PR's own argument explains it and cuts against it — bm25 already down-weights ubiquitous terms, so adding them buys almost no ranking at 5,6x the time. All the value sits in the one degenerate query ('to be or not to be', 25% overlap)
2026-08-29T08:43Z BASELINE PROBE: Zotero's own full-text search on the same twenty queries (bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/zotero-native-baseline.json), answering 'why maintain a lexical layer when the built-in filter exists'. It is SLOWER than both zoteus arms — median 3 216,9 ms, p95 4 198,5 ms, against 392,3 ms stock and 1 773,0 ms stopword-less. It does not RANK: the default order is byte-identical to sort=dateModified while sort=title reorders, so matches come back in modification-date order, and the median query matches 307 items. And it ANDs terms (carbon 4 610 -> carbon tax 1 479 -> +elasticity 214 -> +Vietnam 77), so a longer question narrows to nothing with no partial credit, where OR+bm25 returns its best partial matches. It also returns items, not passages. Conclusion: the lexical layer earns its place through ranking and passage-level results, not through matching — matching is what Zotero already does
2026-08-29T09:16Z DF-DROPLIST, language neutrality and the costs. At a 30% threshold the droplist drops ONLY English function words in this library — measured df: the 84,42%, of 84,96%, then la 9,43%, va 6,99%, le 6,62%, cua 5,15%, des 5,04%, der 0,85%, und 0,46%. That is not the old unfairness: the rule is uniform and the outcome is asymmetric only because the corpus is. English words are dropped because they sit in 84% of passages, so they cost most and discriminate least; 'le' is kept because at 6,6% it is both cheap and informative here. In a French-majority library 'le' would drop and 'the' would not. Costs to weigh: the droplist is corpus-derived state, so ranking shifts silently as the library grows (when it is recomputed, and whether the user is told, are questions the literal list never raised); the threshold is tuned on n=1 and the working window is narrow enough that it may not travel; and it asks the maintainer to own a maintained table where there was a literal
2026-08-29T09:16Z DF-DROPLIST SWEPT on the real index (bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/df-droplist-sweep.json), the author's proposal and the concrete form of DESIGN §3's df-pruning. Direct-SQL timings, comparable among themselves. THE DESIGN'S OWN THRESHOLD IS WRONG: at >=50% df only 9 terms drop and p95 stays at 820,7 ms, 1,6x over the ~500 ms allowance — DESIGN §3 needs correcting. The working window is about 25-35%: at >=30% the droplist catches 23 terms (19 English function words plus '1','2','3','s') and p95 falls to 463,5 ms, while at >=20% it starts eating 'energy' (26,2% df here), which is a catastrophic answer to a query about energy. A hard cutoff is a step function approximating what bm25's idf already does continuously, so it is justified by COST and never by ranking quality — keep the threshold high
2026-08-29T09:16Z DF-DROPLIST, the trap and the policy that works. A df droplist does NOT fix the degenerate query on its own, and neither does an empty-set fallback: at >=30% 'to be or not to be' keeps exactly one term ('not', 27,3% df), so the set is never empty, the fallback never fires, and the stock defect is reproduced exactly (1 term, 10% overlap with the unfiltered query). Fire the fallback on DEGENERACY instead — fewer than two surviving terms sends the raw token set; it fires on 1 of 20. Also firing when more than half the terms were eaten is too aggressive: it caught two healthy queries and pushed p95 to 1 084,8 ms. Rejected. MEASURED POLICY (df>=30% + degeneracy fallback): p50 274,2 ms, p95 536,9 ms, 98% top-30 overlap with the unfiltered query, degenerate query restored to 100% overlap at 914 ms. Against stoplist-stock (365,8 ms p95, 86% overlap, degenerate query at 10%) and no-filter (1 211,7 ms p95, 100%, 100%), it dominates both arms the PR was choosing between
2026-08-29T09:26Z MULTILINGUAL COST MEASURED (bench/results/0025-x2-stopwordless/multilingual-cost.json), five matched queries per language. Multilingualism is not what costs: a French query costs 250 ms where the matched English one costs 1 144 ms, walking 462 606 postings against 8 400 770 — eighteen times cheaper — and Vietnamese is cheaper still at 138 ms. For French and Vietnamese all three policies land within a few ms, because the filter is a no-op for them. What costs is being the MAJORITY language: English function words are expensive because 84% of this corpus is English, and French ones are cheap here for the mirror reason. Invert the corpus and the roles invert
2026-08-29T09:26Z MULTILINGUAL COST, the real price and what it settles. German is the exception in the table and it pays an ENGLISH price: FTS5 indexes strings, not words-of-a-language, so a German function word spelled like an English one inherits the English document frequency. 'in' is the same string in both and sits at 79,01% here, which is the whole of German's 30 ms -> 776 ms swing when the filter is removed; 'was' inherits 16,66%, 'so' 13,67%. French pays it too — 'on' inherits 52,71%, 'or' 30,63%. This is irreducible with one index and one token space. It also settles the SHAPE of the fix independently of any latency number: a per-language stoplist is ill-defined, since dropping German 'die' requires knowing the query is German while 'die' is also English, so the same string must be dropped and kept by an intent the index cannot see. A corpus-derived droplist asks the only well-posed question — what does this string cost in this corpus. Note the irony worth keeping: the English stoplist gives German 30 ms by accident (it contains 'in'), so deleting it in the name of other languages hurts German most; the droplist gives 82 ms. UNMEASURED RISK: minority-language function words become high-idf signals, which interacts badly with homographs (a German query carrying 'die' should rank English passages about mortality). That needs a relevance judgement, not a timer
2026-08-29T09:27Z claude note X1 RECALL HALF MEASURED on real vectors (the author's machine, the substrate X1-recall required). bench/vec_mrl_recall.mjs + bench/vec_task_recall.mjs -> bench/results/0038-mrl-binary/. Not int8 but 1-bit, which subsumes it: at full width, binary codes plus an exact rerank score 0,9973 recall@30 at an 8x pool (Qwen3-0.6B, 1024 dims, 93 022 real passages), against 0,953 at 384 dims in ticket 0008 — binary IMPROVES with width, so the X1 ship decision is a yes wherever the vectors are wide. Scan at #30's geometry (255 703 x 3 072): 4 088,7 ms exact -> 97,2 ms binary, 42x. Two findings beyond X1's question: Matryoshka truncation is a second, multiplying lever (quartering the width costs 4,8% of task recall, not the 22 points rank agreement implies), and the float32 column CANNOT be deleted — reranking narrow scores 0,8603 against 0,9720 full. Driver is anchored on two controls: it reproduces all ten of 0008's figures exactly, and it detects truncation damage on a non-MRL model
2026-08-29T09:50Z FTS5 DOES NOT give the high-df terms for free — the question that decides 0091's shape. fts5vocab is a virtual table over the existing index (no migration, no rebuild, nothing stored), but a df threshold needs a FULL SCAN of it, since the table is ordered by term and not by document count: ~3,4 s warm and 7,5 s cold over 639 888 terms on the real index. Too slow for query time and too slow at every server start, so the droplist is derived state and needs a home. Everything else about it is small — 23 terms and 53 bytes at the 30% threshold, and the meta key/value table already exists. Cadence therefore settles itself: recompute at the end of a full build (263,7 s, so +1,3%), not on every incremental update, rescanning only when the passage count has drifted more than ~10%; an index with no stored droplist filters nothing and adopts one on its first build, the same shape as PR-3's stamp adoption
2026-08-29T10:55Z claude note X1 RECALL, corrections after /gaze ESCALATE on PR 26. (1) The pool multiple was missing and it is load-bearing: the ship verdict holds at the 8x pool, where Qwen3-0.6B at full width reads 0,9973 recall@30 — but nomic-768 reads 0,9710 at 8x, BELOW DESIGN section 3's 0,98 bar, clearing only at 16x (0,9893), still inside the 32x allowance. The rule passes; a reader checking the headline without the pool finds a failure. (2) DESIGN section 3's rule names int8 and this measured 1-bit; the substitution now sits in DECISIONS.md under Awaiting ratification rather than being asserted here, per CLAUDE.md's ledger-first order. Its 400 ms-at-650k clause is UNMEASURED — the ship decision is provisional on a substrate never tested. (3) The scan figures move run to run more than one run admits: three independent invocations at 255 703 x 3 072 gave exact 4 088,7 / 4 196,2 / 4 893,9 ms and binary_3072 97,2 / 94,1 / 87,6 ms, so speedups of 42,1x / 44,6x / 55,9x. The variance is the SQLite scan's, not the codes' — the binary scan is 98 MB resident and reads IQR 2,2%, the exact scan re-reads 3,1 GB. The committed artifact holds the third run (tightest, IQR 2,3%); the upstream comment quotes 42x, the most conservative of the three, and is deliberately not being re-edited upward. (4) bytes_scanned for bigint_3072 was the float32 column's size, 3 142 078 464, because the candidate never registered its own and a fallback substituted a neighbour's — driver fixed, fallback removed, artifact regenerated; it now reads 98 189 952, equal to binary_3072 as it must be since the two scan identical codes. (5) Scope, recorded not ticketed per the severity floor: embed_corpus.py, embed_feasibility.mjs and gpu_feasibility.py (local-embedder throughput) and vec_task_recall.mjs (same-item retrieval, a target outside any one model) rode in under this Ticket-ref without tickets of their own. (6) Artifacts moved from bench/results/0038-mrl-binary to bench/results/0025-x1-recall: 0038 named a ticket that does not exist and sits on the contended frontier fa113ef stepped around; the sibling is 0025-x1-timing
