2026-08-29T13:32Z claude note SCAN BASELINE MISATTRIBUTED, and it is the number the upstream gap was computed against. vec_scan_shapes.mjs's sqlite_float32 candidate is labelled 'what v1.9.0 does' in the driver, the artifact and comment D, but its loop accumulates dot and row norm in ONE traversal with no shared norm() call site — that is the shape PR #31 CREATED, not what v1.9.0 ran (two traversals through a norm() shared with the query-side number[] call, polymorphic for the process's life). Measured directly, three shapes in one process over one SQLite table, all returning the same cosine: v1.9.0 shape 80,826 us/row, two-pass monomorphic 56,889 (1,42x), fused 35,360 (2,29x). The 2,29x agrees with 0070's independent in-situ 2,19x on the real store. CONSEQUENCE: comment D's 'more than twenty' gap to the reporter's 95 s is really about 10x like-for-like (2,29 x 4 088,7 = 9,4 s, or 8,6 s by 0070's two_pass), and the maintainer has carried the ~20x into his closing comment as an open mystery. The 42x speedup is unaffected and is correct against v1.10.0, which is fused; against v1.9.0 it would have been ~88x. Driver relabelled; probe at verification/probes/scan-shape-v190-vs-fused.mjs, artifact at bench/results/0025-x1-recall/scan-shape-attribution.json, report at verification/issue-30-thread.md. The committed scan-shapes artifact still carries the wrong note and needs a re-run at full geometry to clear it. DECISIONS.md quotes these as 'exact scan' in a ratified entry and cannot be edited — needs a new entry, the author's call
2026-08-29T14:26Z claude note V1.9.0 vs V1.10.0 MEASURED end to end on a real index (93 022 passages, 384 dims, REAL vectors, embedder local:Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2; provenance controlled by re-embedding five sampled rows through the same model and getting cosine 1,000000 against the stored slab). Three arms interleaved, 100 warm samples each, mode:semantic: v1.9.0 (bb414df) p50 1 069,1 ms; v1.10.0 with ZOTEUS_INDEX_ANN=false p50 814,6 ms; v1.10.0 stock p50 21,7 ms. 49,3x on the median, reproduced independently at 49,3x. DECOMPOSITION: #31's fused loop 1,31x, the two-stage 37,5x. vectorScan is a real control, not a null: codes on 100/100 warm samples of the default arm and exact on 100/100 of the ANN=false arm. Ranking agreement v1.9.0 exact vs v1.10.0 two-stage: same first hit 20/20, identical top-10 order 17/20, mean overlap 9,65/10. One-time code build about 1,5 s, measured apart from the model-download confound that contaminated the first attempt. mode:auto (the hybrid default a user gets) improves only 12,2x, 1 136,0 -> 93,1 ms p50, because BM25 is untouched. NOT QUOTABLE: any bridge to the reporter's 95 s — this machine's v1.9.0 baseline is 1,07 s where his is ~95 s, his index reads 3,1 GB of vectors per query against our 143 MB, and nothing here explains that gap. 49x is a floor for his geometry, not a prediction. Artifact bench/results/0025-upstream-v190-vs-v1100/, drivers bench/issue30_*, README-issue30.md
2026-08-29T15:25Z claude note MEASUREMENT CAVEAT, verified from the raw samples and material to public quotation: every arm is SLOWER WARM THAN COLD — v1.9.0 963,8 -> 1 069,7 ms, exact 692,7 -> 815,0, two-stage 20,6 -> 21,8, and the drift repeats in run 2 (996,3 -> 1 127,0 / 761,3 -> 834,9 / 20,3 -> 23,0). That is the opposite of cache warming, so something degrades over a ten-minute run; a fanless 15 W i5-8250U throttling is the candidate and nothing measured CPU frequency, so it stays a candidate rather than a verdict. It moves all three arms the same way, so the MEDIAN RATIO is robust (49,18x then 49,11x on my own recomputation from raw) while the p95 ratio is NOT (34,9x then 40,0x, the exact arm's tail moving). Consequence for anything published: quote the median ratio, present the absolute latencies as this machine's, and do not quote a p95 ratio. The agent under-reported this in its first report and surfaced it afterwards
2026-08-29T15:55Z claude note X4-REAL IS NOT RUNNABLE AS DOCUMENTED, and the failure is silent. RUNBOOK step 7 offers 'node bench/constrained_match.mjs <real-index>' as the formality confirming X4 on the real corpus, having verified the driver queries passages_fts by name. That check was necessary and not sufficient: the probe-only path reuses probeQuery(), whose terms are the SYNTHETIC vocabulary ('"w5" OR "w1200" OR "w25000"'). Measured on the real 477 512-passage index at /home/haduong/data/projets/zoteus-bench/x2-rebuild, 2026-08-29: those terms DO exist — 31 matching rows, from OCR debris and variable names — so nothing errors and nothing is empty. The query returns in 12 ms where the synthetic arm reads 3 863 ms at a 1k scope and 731 584 ms at 100k. Run as documented, the confirmation would have reported the constrained step comfortably inside DESIGN section 3's 150 ms allowance and INVERTED X4's no-ship verdict, with no symptom distinguishing it from a genuine pass. The df profile is what the driver's own comment says the cost depends on (one common, one mid, one rare) and it is exactly what a synthetic vocabulary cannot carry into a real corpus: the real index holds 639 888 terms, of which 114 sit at df >= 10 percent, 8 184 between 0,1 and 1 percent, and 128 287 between 5 and 50 documents. The fix is to sample the three bands from fts5vocab on the index under test in probe-only mode, keeping the synthetic path byte-identical so synthetic-477k.json stays reproducible; a full fts5vocab(row) scan costs 16,0 s cold here, paid once per run. NOT DONE in this session and X4-real remains unrun — recorded so the next attempt does not spend the run producing a wrong number. Same family as the vacuous-probe trap already recorded on this ticket: the all-clear and the could-not-look are the same output
2026-08-29T16:14Z claude note X4-REAL, the numbers above corrected against a committed probe. The earlier entry's figures were measured ad hoc through the sqlite3 CLI and with band bounds chosen on the spot; they are superseded by verification/probes/x4_probe_vocabulary.py and its artifact bench/results/0025-x4-constrained-match/real-477k-probe-vocabulary.json. In-process, best of three on the same real 477 512-passage index: the synthetic query costs 1,0 ms where the earlier CLI timing read 12 ms, most of that having been process startup. The finding is unchanged and now carries the control it needed: a query drawn from the three df bands the driver says the cost depends on ('of' at 84,96 percent df, 'steam' at 1,0 percent, '095' at 0,1 percent) costs 379,3 ms on the same index, 379x the synthetic arm. That control could have come out fast and did not, which is what makes the synthetic arm's speed evidence of absent work rather than of performance. Band counts also corrected: 639 888 distinct terms, 114 at df >= 10 percent, 8 195 between 0,1 and 1 percent, 356 124 below 0,1 percent — the earlier '128 287 in 5 to 50 documents' counted a different band and is not comparable. Vocabulary scan 5,4 s warm against the 16,0 s cold quoted before. RUNBOOK step 7 now carries the artifact's numbers and the probe path; step 6 was removed as already-measured and step 4's stale v1.9.0 trunk premise was corrected to read UPSTREAM at run time
2026-08-29T16:46Z claude note X4 REAL-CORPUS ARM MEASURED and the verdict holds (bench/results/0025-x4-constrained-match/real-477k.json, host doudou, the real 477 512-passage index at x2-rebuild, 20 probes per scope, terms drawn from the index's own fts5vocab in three df bands — 98/377/385 usable terms, samples 'of'/'steam'/'co2eq', 6,1 s scan). Unconstrained over the whole corpus: median 73,6 ms, p95 362,4 ms. At a 1000-rowid scope: median 585,7 ms, p95 11 969,6 ms — the p95 is 80x DESIGN section 3's 150 ms allowance, so per the rule NO CONSTRAINED STEP SHIPS and the ladder ends at R18's give-up, exactly as the synthetic arm concluded. Two things the real corpus adds beyond confirmation. First, scoping is 8x SLOWER than not scoping (585,7 ms median constrained against 73,6 ms unconstrained), so the finding is not that scoping is expensive but that it is worse than ranking everything, on real text as on synthetic. Second, superlinearity reproduces: 5x the scope costs 7,5x the median (4 406,2 ms at 5k, p95 74 577,3 ms). The 20k and 100k rungs were deliberately NOT run — the rule turns on the smallest scope and 1k already fails it by 80x, so the larger rungs would only redraw a curve already shown superlinear, at 74,6 s per probe and worse. DRIVER FIXED to make this runnable: constrained_match.mjs's probe-only path now samples the three df bands from fts5vocab on the index under test instead of reusing the synthetic Zipf vocabulary; the synthetic path is untouched and was positive-controlled by replaying the old generator against the new one on the same seed — 25 of 25 queries identical, so synthetic-477k.json stays reproducible. SCOPES are now overridable via X4_SCOPES so a run can be bounded to the rung that decides the verdict
2026-08-29T17:25Z claude note YEAR SCOPING MEASURED and X4's verdict does not bind it (bench/year_scope.mjs, bench/harvest_years.py, artifact bench/results/0025-year-scope/year-vs-json-each.json, real 477 512-passage index, 2026-08-29). The author asked for search scoped by years and the X4 result had been reported to him as if it closed that door. It does not. X4 measured ONE mechanism, an arbitrary rowid set through json_each, which is what a collection or a tag needs because their membership is not stored. A year is a stored attribute, so it is a column with an index and an ordinary predicate. THE FINDING THAT COMES FIRST is that the shipped index stores no date at all — items is (item_key, title), passages carries none — so year scoping today is not slow, it is impossible, and no latency verdict was ever the obstacle; the years here were harvested from the local API's meta.parsedDate (7 426 of 7 541 items carry one, spanning 1622 to 2026) and written to a COPY. Adding the column plus two indexes over the whole index cost 529 ms. MEASURED, each scope run BOTH ways over the same items and the same queries: one year (2020, 17 422 passages) costs 254,8 ms median by predicate against 11 141,9 ms by json_each — 43x — and against this run's own unconstrained baseline of 207 ms the filter is free to within noise, with five years at 181,3 ms and a decade at 196,1 ms both CHEAPER than no filter, because the predicate removes work. The control is what makes this a finding rather than a hope: it could have come out equal and it came out 43x apart, so the cost is the mechanism and not the scoping. The json_each arm was run only at the smallest scope and is recorded as SKIPPED with its reason at the larger two, where it would re-measure X4's superlinear curve for hours. WHAT IT DOES NOT SHOW: nothing about collections or tags, which stay X4's territory, and nothing about the give-up frequency when a narrow scope empties the ranked stream. Ledger entry filed awaiting ratification; the upstream half — zotero_semantic_search takes no filter arguments at all (only q, limit, mode, auto_build) and the index has no date to filter on — is an upstream ask and is NOT filed
