2026-08-30T07:06Z claude note X6 MEASURED, PARTIALLY -- one arm only, headless (bench/results/0025-x6-version-dynamics/x6-verdict.json). Zotero was confirmed running before any step (127.0.0.1:23119); the never-synced-profile arm needs a second live Zotero instance, which the run's own hard constraints (no GUI, no second instance) forbid -- not attempted, recorded as a limitation rather than silently skipped. Available arm: attachment 8YHZTSKL (IEA Sankey Vietnam 2020.pdf, smallest non-empty ft-cache in the census, chosen to minimize footprint), .zotero-ft-cache deleted -- the only mutation authorized. Before: library v418, item v0, census entry 13780. Polled the item's /fulltext endpoint every 10 s for 5 minutes (30 polls): immediately 404 after deletion, still 404 at every poll, no spontaneous re-extraction. After 5 minutes: library v418, item v0, census entry 13780, census population unchanged at 8037 -- NOTHING in the version/census machinery moved. DECISION RULE (DESIGN section 3): cannot be evaluated as written -- the rule asks what re-extraction does to the version signals ('re-stamps 0' vs 'bumps something'), and re-extraction never occurred headless in the observation window (deleting the cache forces unavailability, not re-extraction; that needs the PDF opened in the reader or a UI Reindex, both excluded here). Reported as an open question, not a null result, per the workflow's positive-control discipline. WHAT IS SETTLED instead: the fulltext-content endpoint and the version/census machinery are NOT coupled -- content can go from servable to 404 with every version signal (library, item, and the item's own census entry) reporting business as usual. This bears on I-1/0024's freshness design as evidence FOR the md5-widened-signal proposal on the #26 thread being necessary but not sufficient alone -- an md5 of the SOURCE file would not catch this case either, since only the derived cache was touched. Side effect: item 8YHZTSKL has no extracted fulltext until the author re-opens it or runs Reindex Item (trivial, low-value single-page infographic, deliberately chosen).
