2026-08-30T09:17Z claude note X3a WALL-TIME GAP (182,2 s vs 371,6 s), REASONING ARM -- descoped from a repeated A/B by the author mid-run ('the code-diff reasoning arm alone satisfies the question'); one measurement had already started and is folded in below as a labelled aside, not as the requested experiment. PROVENANCE OF THE OLD FIGURE, established because bench/results/0011-rss/uncapped-build-3.json records no SHA at all: it is NOT an upstream zoteus SHA. STATE.md as of 2026-08-21 says fork/ then sat on branch fts5-base (PR #11 + PR #12 merged, not upstream main -- this project's OWN SQLite/FTS5 prototype, a 'PassageStore port under the one existing SearchIndex'), and SYNC.md's 2026-08-26 entry names that state as fork commit bae82a7; the fork's remote still carries it archived as origin/archive/fts5-storage-2026-08-21, tip bae82a7, dated 2026-08-22 00:05:23+02:00, hours before 0011's run that same day -- bound with high confidence, not certainty, since the artifact itself is silent. STRUCTURAL FINDING, framing every candidate below: bae82a7 and b132f2d are NOT two points on one lineage -- git merge-base bae82a7 b132f2d = 40bccc5, and git merge-base --is-ancestor bae82a7 b132f2d is false. They are sibling implementations of a SQLite/FTS5 backend diverging from a common pre-SQLite ancestor: bae82a7 is this repo's own abandoned prototype, b132f2d descends from the maintainer's own from-scratch rewrite (SearchIndex interface + SqliteSearchIndex, merged upstream as eee1000/#17 on 2026-08-25, three days AFTER 0011 ran). So there is no `git log old..new` commit range to walk; the candidates below come from reading the two trees side by side (`git -C fork show bae82a7:<path>` against the b132f2d checkout). CANDIDATES, ranked by fit to a ~2x factor. RANK 1, persist/commit cadence widened specifically for the full-text phase: NEW fork/src/features/search/build.ts:404 sets `persistEveryItemsFulltext: 500, persistEveryMsFulltext: 60_000` only when `wantFulltext`; index-manager.ts:691-697 resolves those per-phase, falling back to the base 200/10_000 only absent an override; index-manager.ts:792-793 selects the fulltext pair whenever `this.phase === 'fulltext'`; sqlite-index.ts:481-489 commits one BEGIN/COMMIT pair per persist() call. OLD (bae82a7) index-manager.ts:761-762 sets a single flat `persistEveryItems ?? 200` / `persistEveryMs ?? 10_000` uniformly across metadata AND full-text passes -- no per-phase override exists in this tree at all; fts5-store.ts:820-838 (beginBatch/commitBatch) is the same BEGIN/COMMIT mechanism. Mechanism: at the roughly 19-26 items/s this build runs, OLD's 200-item/10s ceiling fires about every 8-10s (~28-38 commits over the 7541-item build); NEW's 500-item/60s ceiling is item-bound at this rate (~20-26s per 500 items, under the 60s cap) and fires about every 20-26s (~11-15 commits) -- roughly 2,5x fewer WAL-commit/status-poll boundaries, concentrated in exactly the full-text-dominated phase this experiment measures. NEW's own comment at index-manager.ts:695-697 names the SQLite cost of skipping this as merely 'a slightly longer transaction', which reads as an under-estimate given the gap measured here. Weak corroboration: today's NEW run peaked at 2 046,1 MiB against OLD-today's 1 990,5 MiB (+2,8%), consistent with holding more uncommitted passages between larger, less frequent commits, though 2,8% sits inside this ticket's own documented run-to-run noise (X1 recall entry: three runs spread 4088,7-4893,9 ms) and is not proof alone. RANK 2, unquantified: schema shape changed from 'passages IS the FTS5 virtual table' to 'a plain passages table plus a separate passages_fts index'. OLD fts5-store.ts:317 `INSERT INTO passages(body)` (passages is itself FTS5) plus fts5-store.ts:318-319 a companion `passage_meta(rowid, id, item, ord, title, source)` row; NEW sqlite-index.ts:417-420 `insertPassage` into a plain `passages(id, item_key, title, text, source)` plus a separate `insertFts` into `passages_fts(rowid, text)`. This is the exact pre/post-rename pair this ticket's own 2026-08-29 entries already named (SUBSTRATE MAP CORRECTION / Precision on the previous entry), corroborating bae82a7 as the tree those entries meant. Both shapes issue two INSERTs per passage, so a naive statement count does not favour either side, and no measurement here isolates its per-row cost -- ranked below RANK 1 because it is not even directionally established from reading alone. RULED OUT: chunker.ts is BYTE-IDENTICAL between bae82a7 and b132f2d (`diff` empty) -- the chunking algorithm and passage-count geometry are not a candidate (both trees produce 477 511 passages / 465 109 fulltext on today's library, matching to the unit). fulltext-source.ts's extraction strategy (two library-wide reads, then one GET per attachment with text, createFulltextSource) is unchanged in shape between the trees, inherited near-verbatim from the shared ancestor 40bccc5 -- not a candidate. WAL journal_mode + synchronous=NORMAL are set in both (fts5-store.ts:305-306 old; sqlite-index.ts:262,270 new) -- not a candidate. MEASUREMENT ASIDE, not the requested A/B (started before the descope landed; author authorized letting it finish): OLD-SHA (bae82a7) rebuilt TODAY on host doudou, same live library, same driver, same conditions as X3a's own NEW-SHA run this morning (entry above) -- `ZOTEUS_SEARCH_BACKEND=sqlite python3 bench/run_build.py --server fork/dist/index.js --data-dir <fresh mktemp -d> --backend sqlite --build --max-items 1000000 --max-chars 0` (bae82a7 has no ZOTEUS_INDEX_BACKEND var, predating that rename, so the sqlite backend was selected via its own pre-rename env var, exported ambient so run_build.py's env dict does not shadow it). Result: elapsed_s 293,2, peak_rss_kb 2 038 316 = 1 990,5 MiB, 477 511 passages / 465 109 fulltext -- the same exact geometry as this morning's NEW-SHA run. Raw log left at /tmp (ephemeral, not committed -- the descope said create no bench/results/ artifact for this arm). This ONE run lets the 371,6-vs-182,2s gap be decomposed, tautologically but usefully: same-day/same-host/code-only factor = 293,2 / 182,2 = 1,61x; same-code/different-day residual = 371,6 / 293,2 = 1,27x; product = 2,04x, the original gap exactly. So some of the gap is genuinely version-side (the 1,61x, isolated from host/day) and some is plausibly host/day/cache warmth (the 1,27x) -- but each arm is a single run, and this ticket's own X1 entries record ~10-20% run-to-run spread on a much shorter operation, so the 1,27x residual could itself be partly noise rather than a distinct day effect; not strong enough to promote to a committed artifact or a ratified figure. VERDICT: the observation stands, 182,2 s (b132f2d) vs 371,6 s (the OLD figure, provenance above); the cause is NOT isolated by a controlled instrumented measurement -- no per-commit timing was captured on either tree. Version-side is now better supported than before this entry (the one-run same-day decomposition above puts a code-only factor at 1,61x, most of the observed 2,04x), and RANK 1's persist-cadence change is the best-fitting NAMED mechanism, but it is not proven to BE the 1,61x -- a plausible, well-cited candidate, not an isolated cause. Honest verdict per the repo's diagnosis discipline: version-side, mechanism not isolated; RANK 1 is the leading candidate. The ONE experiment that would settle it: instrument index-manager.ts's persistNow()/maybePersist() (or sqlite-index.ts's begin()/commit()) with a per-commit timestamp on both trees, rerun the same uncapped build on each, and read off total time inside BEGIN..COMMIT against total wall time -- if RANK 1 is the mechanism the two totals should move by close to the same 1,5-2x the wall clock does. Not run here, per the descope.
